{% extends "shell.njk" %}{% block body %}<h1>Chargeback vs showback: IT cost allocation</h1>
<p class="updated" style="color:#6B7689;font-size:14px;margin-top:-6px">Updated 23 October 2026</p>
<p>IT chargeback and showback are two ways to allocate technology costs back to the teams that consume them. Both improve cost awareness, but only one moves budget responsibility. This guide covers what each is, how they compare, why showback usually comes first, and when to switch.</p>

<h2>What is IT chargeback?</h2>
<p>Chargeback bills business units directly for the IT services they use. If engineering runs workloads that cost $50,000 in a month, engineering's budget is charged $50,000. The team now owns the cost and has a direct incentive to optimize it.</p>
<p>Chargeback is common in cloud FinOps because usage is measurable. Every compute instance, storage bucket, API call, and model request can be tagged to a team, project, or product.</p>
<p><strong>Chargeback works when</strong> usage is measurable per team, the team controls the cost driver, allocation data is trusted, and finance is ready to move budget lines.</p>

<h2>What is IT showback?</h2>
<p>Showback reports the same usage data to business units without transferring the bill. Engineering still sees that it consumed $50,000, but the central IT budget pays it. The goal is transparency and behavior change, not budget transfer.</p>
<p><strong>Showback works when</strong> data is still directional, costs are shared across teams, workloads are new (AI pilots, for example), or the organization is early in its FinOps practice.</p>

<h2>Chargeback vs showback compared</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Chargeback</th><th>Showback</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Budget owner</td><td>Consuming team</td><td>Central IT</td></tr>
<tr><td>Incentive</td><td>Strong, immediate</td><td>Informational, social</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation effort</td><td>High: procurement, budgeting and finance changes</td><td>Low: reports and dashboards</td></tr>
<tr><td>Data quality needed</td><td>Audit-grade</td><td>Directional</td></tr>
<tr><td>Political friction</td><td>High if teams pay for what they cannot control</td><td>Low</td></tr>
<tr><td>Best FinOps maturity</td><td>Run</td><td>Inform / Optimize</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical first use case</td><td>Compute, storage, LLM API usage</td><td>Shared platforms, AI pilots</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>Why showback comes first</h2>
<p>Showback is the proving ground for chargeback. Before money moves, it answers four questions: is the tagging complete, can shared costs be split fairly, do the numbers survive seasonality, and do teams change behavior when they see their spend?</p>
<p>Running showback first surfaces the problems that would make chargeback contested:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tagging gaps.</strong> Untagged resources show up as an "unallocated" line that someone has to own.</li>
<li><strong>Shared-service disputes.</strong> Teams argue about how platform, network and support costs are split, before it affects their budget.</li>
<li><strong>Seasonality.</strong> A month of batch jobs or a launch spike looks different when you have several months to compare.</li>
<li><strong>Behavioral surprises.</strong> You learn which teams respond to reports and which need a harder incentive.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Implementing showback in four steps</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Tag.</strong> Attach team, project and environment to every resource and API key.</li>
<li><strong>Dashboard.</strong> Build one view per team from the tagged billing data.</li>
<li><strong>Distribute.</strong> Send the report monthly, then weekly once the data is stable.</li>
<li><strong>Educate.</strong> Walk each team through what drives its number and what it can change.</li>
</ol>
<p>A useful showback report fits on one screen: spend with a comparison to last period, one unit metric (cost per request, per user or per feature), the top three cost drivers, and one recommended action. Send it to the engineering team that owns the workload and copy the budget holder; reports sent only to finance rarely change behavior.</p>

<h2>When to move to chargeback</h2>
<p>A typical migration runs month 0–1 for visibility, months 2–4 for showback, and month 5 onward for hybrid chargeback. Plan for at least two to three showback cycles. You are ready when:</p>
<ul>
<li>Under 5% of allocated cost is disputed.</li>
<li>Showback has run for at least two months without method changes.</li>
<li>Finance has agreed the allocation method and the budget transfers.</li>
<li>Each team has identified levers it can actually pull.</li>
<li>Leadership will enforce the result when a team objects.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><strong>Start chargeback narrow.</strong> Charge back one controllable, well-tagged cost (for example a team's direct LLM API usage) and keep everything else on showback. Widen it once the first line runs cleanly.</div>

<h2>Hybrid is the destination</h2>
<p>Most mature organizations do not pick one model. They charge back stable, controllable services and show back shared platform costs. For AI, that usually means showback for the base LLM platform (gateways, vector stores, observability) and chargeback for direct API usage that a team's product decisions drive.</p>

<h2>Applying this to AI and LLM spend</h2>
<p>AI workloads make chargeback and showback more important, not less. LLM costs are granular, spiky, and driven by product usage. Without allocation, a single team's experiment can double the AI budget overnight. For the LLM-specific angle, see our guide to <a href="/research/llm-chargeback-showback">LLM chargeback and showback</a>.</p>
<p>For the finance and governance perspective, see the <a href="https://llmcfo.com/research/ai-chargeback-showback" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LLM CFO guide to AI chargeback and showback</a>.</p>

